2186045417] [17592186558477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558477 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558477 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558477 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558477 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "feec1631-31d9-531c-849d-52f889b504d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558483 #edb/ref 1071 "The population of an ant colony grew exponentially, and within $4$ months, the population was $1\\,000\\cdot (1.5)^{4}.$ What does the expression $(1.5)^{4}$ mean in this context?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "087c8650-e701-5898-adf4-3590d90adcb5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558483 #edb/ref 1074 "The given expression is an exponential growth expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t}$, where:\n\n- $a=1\\,000$ represents the initial population of ants\n\n- $b=1.5$ is the growth factor\n\n- $t=4$ is the time in months since the start of the observations\n\nFor the factor $(1.5)^{4},$ the growth rate $(1.5)$ means that the population grows by $50%$ per month, and the exponent $(4)$ gives the number of months. \n\nTherefore, the ant population grew by a factor of $(1.5)^{4}$ over the first $4$ months." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558496 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the real solutions to the equation $5(x-8)^{8}=1280.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f819e43-7e0b-5558-81ee-7d5d86950aea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558496 #edb/ref 1074 "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x-8)^{8}$ term and then taking the $8$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x-8)^{8}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x-8)^{8} & =1280 \\\\ (x-8)^{8} & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $8$th root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since the exponent $(8)$ is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-8)^{8} & =256 \\\\ \\sqrt[8]{(x-8)^{8}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[8]{256} \\\\ x-8 & =\\pm \\sqrt[8]{{2}^{8}} \\\\ x-8 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =8\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=6$ and $x=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-22491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558498 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $6{(\\frac{2a}{3})}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21ebb6b3-86f2-53e7-bae9-ebede4e1482d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558498 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{(\\frac{2a}{3})}^{3}=6\\cdot \\frac{(2a)^{3}}{{3}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power of product rule and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{(2a)^{3}}{{3}^{3}} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{3}{a}^{3}}{{3}^{3}} & = \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{8{a}^{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{48{a}^{3}}{27} & = \\\\ \\frac{16{a}^{3}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558500 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $2{r}^{2}+16r+32.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "275b1fab-422b-52b6-b4bd-24f3217dcda4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558500 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{r}^{2}+16r+32 & = \\\\ 2({r}^{2}+8r+16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2}+8r+16 & = \\\\ {r}^{2}+8r+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ {r}^{2}+2\\cdot 4r+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ (r+4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({r}^{2}+8r+16) & = \\\\ 2(r+4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558531 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\text{LCM}(75,20,15)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a0d5183-c49d-5331-96ab-591f74bbcbc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558531 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $75:$\n\n![](images/29/292ce7efadbf5b6797dc8fabe6efa2689ba3d000fcf00347f29b5f03f4cf5f3c.png)\n\nTherefore, $75=3\\cdot {5}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $20:$\n\n![](images/7e/7e03a139fc3afa7df4d7f32ecf42f8f30d19bfe0415725f47fe5be63301899b5.png)\n\nTherefore, $20={2}^{2}\\cdot 5.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $15.$ We easily deduce that $15=3\\cdot 5.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}75 & =3\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 20 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 15 & =3\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $2$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $75$, $20$  and $15$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(75,20,15)={2}^{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {5}^{2}=300.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558541 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558541 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558541 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558541 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in descending powers of $m$ and ascending powers of $p,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(m-5p)}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "974e81bd-58de-5f99-a98d-0d5e2c335ae7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558541 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=m,$ and $b=-5p.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(m-5p)}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(m)^{3}(-5p)^{0}+3(m)^{2}(-5p)^{1}+3(m)^{1}(-5p)^{2}+1(m)^{0}(-5p)^{3} \\\\  & =1({m}^{3})(1)+3({m}^{2})(-5p)+3(m)(25{p}^{2})+1(1)(-125{p}^{3}) \\\\  & ={m}^{3}-15{m}^{2}p+75m{p}^{2}-125{p}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $m$ and ascending powers of $p,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{m}^{3}-15{m}^{2}p+75m{p}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558559 #edb/ref 1071 "An adult says $11\\,400$ words in $60$ minutes. If we want to determine the average of words spoken per minute, we use the formula $P=\\frac{w}{t},$ being $w$ the total number of words and $t$ the time in minutes.\n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bae6efb2-0046-5ab6-af30-8194a5982502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558559 #edb/ref 1074 "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $11\\,400$ and $60.$ They do not matter, if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $w$ represents $\\text{words}$ and $t$ represents $\\text{minute},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $P=\\frac{w}{t}$ is $\\frac{\\text{words}}{\\text{minute}},$ that is, 'words per minute'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558562 #edb/ref 1071 "A car rental agency charges a fixed fee of $$25$ plus $$0.08$ per mile for the rental of a particular car.\n\nWhat expression gives the total cost, in dollars, if the car is driven for  $m$ miles?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c762ecb0-e32e-5090-9c36-4c8778229991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558562 #edb/ref 1074 "If $m$ is the number of miles that the car is driven, and the car rental agency charges a fee of $$0.08$ per mile, then the cost for $m$ miles must be $0.08m.$ \n\nThere is also a fixed fee of $$25.$ Consequently, the constant term of the expression should be $25.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost (in dollars) when the car is driven for  $m$ miles must be \n\n$$\n0.08m+25\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558568 #edb/ref 1071 "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(4,-1)$ and $(-2,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5f6ff3b-f39c-5102-ab5a-34006293061b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558568 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the line passes through the point $(4,-1),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,-1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-4-(-1)}{-2-4)}=\\frac{-3}{-6}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,-1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system  of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=3 \\\\ x-y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2633cd2-d747-5eed-ad0b-333971ef5a97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558573 #edb/ref 1074 "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ into $x-y=1,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03186a3d-c5ee-569a-a03d-2b6a3c51d524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11ad8382-e548-54e8-8797-9de5e2d6db1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1bc2cdcc-6358-53c6-aabf-fcab9b35aa60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558591 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c1b618d-aae5-5ea0-95a9-b46b784b8741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558600 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558600 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24cac7e7-efc1-566c-a817-79333c9bfd52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558600 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "396d86b8-1505-53aa-9124-4546fecbaee3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558620 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558620 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52894ffc-4454-536c-82ee-5b920e9bd6a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558620 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "625689c5-7219-5fe2-95ce-d90205ef1868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558648 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dc9c66a-b07c-5e94-9816-f7a50fb0d888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94a757d5-404c-567b-b639-e95446077c0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558681 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558681 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba3c538d-4be0-5fa1-aad7-7eb962e8f9ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558681 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb11de92-084a-57f2-b3da-26bfa73e05a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558685 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1ad8542-52af-5573-9dd5-00ac5b8806c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558693 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d27593e5-46d5-5615-a52c-7eea72d15309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558705 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea9c4816-ac6a-5bcd-8e6d-e9fca0874b38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558715 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186558648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558715 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffda8c96-daa1-5849-a03c-4fd7a2a82010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558715 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186558573 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
